### Chart 4A: HTTP-Native Per-Request Payment Challenge, Signed Authorization & Single-Use Settlement

| Evidence Field | Required Finding |
| --- | --- |
| **SSPS reference** | **Mint-to Logic™** (April 15, 2025 formal stage drawings — Stage `[100]` Source Input, Stage `[200]` Validation/Trigger, Stage `[300]` Mint Unit Container, Stage `[400]` Routing Decision Layer, Stage `[500a]` Burn, Stage `[500b]` Limbo, Stage `[500c]` Access, Stage `[500d]` Code, and Stage `[600]` Audit Trail) and **The Shepherd's Method™ / Process** (December 19, 2024 provisional filing baseline — pre-execution authorization gating and boundary control). |
| **External product** | **x402 Protocol** (`x402-foundation/x402` Specification v2, HTTP `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` / `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` / `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` headers, Facilitator `/verify` & `/settle` interface) paired with **Google AP2 / A2A x402 Extension** (`google-agentic-commerce/a2a-x402` v0.1 / v0.2 signed mandates). |
| **Input** | An unauthenticated/unpaid HTTP request (e.g., `GET /resource`) or A2A task request from an AI agent or client without pre-existing accounts, subscriptions, or static API keys, followed on retry by a Base64-encoded `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` header (and optional AP2 signed checkout/payment mandate). |
| **Validation** | **Resource Server & Facilitator (`POST /verify`):** Validates cryptographic signature authenticity, payer address, scheme match (e.g., `exact`), CAIP-2 blockchain network (e.g., `eip155:84532`, Solana), atomic token amount, merchant `payTo` destination, and expiration window (`maxTimeoutSeconds`), returning `isValid` / `invalidReason`. Note: balance and nonce replay checks are finalized at settlement time. |
| **State artifact** | **x402:** Signed, non-custodial, recipient-locked payment authorization payload (`PaymentPayload` referencing `PaymentRequirements`, e.g., EIP-3009 authorization on EVM or SPL `TransferChecked` on Solana) plus on-chain settlement receipt (`SettlementResponse` with `transaction` hash). **AP2:** Cryptographically signed user intent/cart/payment mandates proving human delegation. |
| **Routing** | Server middleware selects accepted payment schemes (`exact`, metered/capped), CAIP-2 settlement networks, asset contracts (primarily USDC stablecoins), and facilitator endpoints; facilitator routes verified payloads to the target blockchain RPC without taking custody of client funds. |
| **Execution** | Facilitator broadcasts and settles the signed token transfer on-chain (`POST /settle`), while the resource server fulfills the gated API call, data fetch, or A2A/MCP agent capability and returns `200 OK` with a `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` header. |
| **Terminal behavior** | **On completion:** On-chain settlement permanently consumes the signed authorization and nonce so it cannot be replayed (a strong functional parallel to single-use consumption / `[500a]` Burn), while leaving no built-in protocol-level chargeback/dispute escrow. **On rejection/timeout:** Returns `HTTP 402 Payment Required` (`PAYMENT-REQUIRED` challenge hold) or error reason (e.g., `settlement_pending` or invalid payload) if payment is missing, expired past `maxTimeoutSeconds`, or underfunded. |
| **Evidence** | HTTP `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` settlement confirmation header (`success`, `payer`, `transaction` hash, `network`, `amount`), immutable public blockchain transaction record, and optional AP2 signed mandate linkage. |
| **Chronology** | **Technical document date:** x402 initial specification published May 6, 2025; Google AP2 and A2A x402 extension published September 2025; x402 Specification v2 published December 2025. **Release date:** Coinbase launched x402 May 6, 2025; Google launched AP2 September 2025 (governed by FIDO Alliance since April 2026); Linux Foundation launched the x402 Foundation in July 2026. **Documented implementation date:** `coinbase/x402` and `x402-foundation/x402` Go/TypeScript/Python SDKs and `google-agentic-commerce/a2a-x402` v0.1–v0.2 repositories implemented across May 2025–2026. **Demonstrated functional behavior date:** Post-dates both the December 19, 2024 SSPS™ filing baseline and April 15, 2025 Mint-to Logic™ drawings (while building on the dormant 1997 HTTP 402 status code and EIP-3009). |
| **Relationship** | **Partial / Analogous** across pre-action signature verification (`[200]`), self-contained single-request signed authorization payloads (`[300]`), single-use nonce/settlement consumption (`[500a]`), HTTP 402 challenge holds (`[500b]`), per-request pay-to-access gating (`[500c]`), and immutable settlement receipts (`[600]`); **Absent** for non-financial multi-branch state routing (`[400]`), timer-driven Limbo auto-escalation/burn (`[500b]`), and wallet-level spend caps (intentionally delegated to the application/wallet layer). |

### Key Engineering & Interoperability Takeaways for ONE & Spendzar

* **Separation of Permission vs. Settlement:** Separating user authorization mandates (**AP2**, governed by FIDO Alliance since April 2026) from per-request settlement (**x402 v2**, governed by the Linux Foundation's x402 Foundation since July 2026) clarifies how **Spendzar** and **ONE** can isolate human spend policies and escrow/dispute protections from the underlying transport rail.
* **Chronology & Scope:** Both x402 (May 6, 2025) and AP2 (September 2025) post-date your **December 19, 2024** filing baseline and **April 15, 2025** Mint-to Logic™ drawings, while intentionally leaving wallet spend limits, multi-branch state transitions, and chargeback/dispute arbitration to higher-level governance systems.

